---
// The masthead of a case-study header: the wordmark, and the way back to the index. The figure
// beside it is side-aware, so the line still says which half of the practice the page sat on —
// but it says it in the drawing, not in words. See the annotation note below for the swap.
//
// The panel head used to open with the logo and then this line, so the figure was drawn
// TWICE, stacked, ~30px apart, at two sizes. The logo was there to be the link home; this
// line was there to name the side. One of them had to go (chooser andrewshiau-panel-marks
// Q5/06), and the marker is the one that carried a fact, so it took the link as well. That
// merge is what left a link reading "Design + Build" — the defect the annotation caught.
//
// It draws `MarkFigure` side-aware: the whole figure where the page sat on both halves of the
// practice, the ring alone for design, the disc alone for build. A pairing is only drawn where
// there is one — the diagonal's whole subject is two things joined, and on `/work/luthier/`
// there is no second thing for the rule to reach.
//
// SAME GLYPH AS THE INDEX ROW, at 26px instead of 18. The index used to draw a flat 22×8
// version, so this file's note read "the logo's figure, not the flat glyph the index uses";
// that glyph is gone (chooser: andrewshiau-mark-options, Q3/05 — see SideDot.astro). The two
// wrappers now differ in size, hover, and what the text beside the figure says. It is not
// SideDot itself because that one prints the side word (clipped) and is not a link; both read
// their side from the same `sideOf`, so the figure can't drift from the index's.
//
// IT READS THE NAME NOW, NOT THE SIDE (annotation: "i think design + build doesn't add a lot
// here and we have no link to get back to the main page. can it isntead be [logomark thing]
// Andrew Shiau and clicking goes back to top").
//
// The link home was already here — this element has been the way back since Q5/06 — but it said
// "Design + Build", so no reader could tell. A link whose text names something other than its
// destination is not discoverable, and the annotation is the proof: the person who built it
// reported the site had no way home. The accessible name was right ("andrewshiau — home") and
// only a screen reader ever got it, which is the tell that the visible text was the bug.
// So the text is the wordmark, and the label a link needs is the one it now prints. The
// `aria-label` is deleted rather than corrected: with "Andrew Shiau" as the text there is
// nothing left for it to add, and an aria-label that restates the text is one more string that
// can drift from what's on screen.
//
// THE SIDE WORD IS GONE, not moved to a tooltip. The annotation's judgment is that it doesn't
// earn its place at the top of a case study, and the figure beside it still carries the fact
// visually — ring for design, disc for build, both joined for both. Putting the word behind a
// `title` would be hiding it rather than removing it, and the win is the thing that's gone.
// The index made the same call for a different reason (Q1/05: eight rows repeating "Design +
// Build" was the loudest thing in that column) but kept the word clipped in the a11y tree,
// because there the marker column is the only place the side is stated at all. Here the ledger
// under the title already names the artifact type, and the side is not a fact this page owes a
// reader before the title.
import { sideOf } from '../data/experiments';
import MarkFigure from './MarkFigure.astro';
interface Props { href: string }
const { href } = Astro.props;
const side = sideOf(href);
---
<p class="smark">
  <a class="smark-home" href="/"><MarkFigure side={side} />Andrew Shiau</a>
</p>
